  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Peripheral nerves run from the spinal cord to the arms and legs. Along the way they pass through natural "tunnels": at the wrist, the elbow, the knee and the ankle. If a tunnel narrows — because of swelling, a thickened ligament, a cyst, injury or constant pressure — the nerve gets trapped. First its blood supply suffers, then its conduction, and with prolonged compression the structure of the nerve itself.</p>
    <p>That is why entrapment syndromes develop gradually: numbness at night first, then during the day, then weakness and muscle wasting. The last stage is not always reversible.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="vidy">
    <h2>Types</h2>
    <h3>In the arm</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/carpal-tunnel-syndrome/"><strong>Carpal tunnel syndrome</strong></a> — the median nerve at the wrist. The thumb, index and middle fingers go numb. The most common entrapment syndrome.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/cubital-tunnel-syndrome/"><strong>Cubital tunnel syndrome</strong></a> — the ulnar nerve at the elbow. The little and ring fingers go numb, especially when the arm is bent for long.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/guyon-canal-syndrome/"><strong>Guyon canal syndrome</strong></a> — the same ulnar nerve, but at the wrist. The little finger is numb and small hand muscles weaken.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>In the leg</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/peroneal-nerve-entrapment/"><strong>Peroneal nerve entrapment</strong></a> — at the knee. The outer shin is numb and the foot "slaps" when walking.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/tarsal-tunnel-syndrome/"><strong>Tarsal tunnel syndrome</strong></a> — the tibial nerve at the ankle. Burning and numbness of the sole.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/treatment/nerves/meralgia-paresthetica/"><strong>Meralgia paresthetica</strong></a> — a thigh nerve under the inguinal ligament. Burning and numbness of the outer thigh.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A separate topic is <a href="/en/treatment/nerves/nerve-injuries/">nerve injuries from trauma and cuts</a>: there, weeks count.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="prichiny">
    <h2>Other causes of numbness</h2>
    <p>Not every numbness is an entrapment syndrome. Similar symptoms are caused by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>disc herniations in the cervical or lumbar spine — a nerve root is compressed;</li>
      <li>polyneuropathy — with diabetes, vitamin B deficiency, alcohol misuse;</li>
      <li>vascular disease, anaemia;</li>
      <li>disorders of the brain or spinal cord.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If numbness appears suddenly, together with weakness of the arm and leg on one side or speech problems, it may be a stroke. Then you need an ambulance, not a routine appointment.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination</strong> — the area of numbness and which muscles are weak usually show which nerve is affected and where.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction studies and EMG</strong> — measure how fast impulses travel along the nerve. They show exactly where the nerve is compressed and how badly.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve ultrasound</strong> — shows swelling of the nerve before the narrowing, cysts and other causes of compression.</li>
      <li><strong>MRI</strong> — when indicated: to find a mass pressing on the nerve or to rule out a spinal disc herniation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p><strong>Without surgery</strong> — for mild compression and recent symptoms: change the load, avoid the habitual posture that compresses the nerve, a night splint, exercise therapy. Medication is prescribed by a doctor. Hand exercises are <a href={`${tg}/28`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">on my Telegram channel</a> (in Russian).</p>
    <p><strong>Surgery</strong> — nerve decompression: the ligament or tissue compressing the nerve is divided and the nerve is freed. It is considered if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>non-surgical treatment has not helped for several months;</li>
      <li>weakness or muscle wasting has appeared;</li>
      <li>nerve conduction studies show severe compression;</li>
      <li>the nerve is compressed by a cyst, a tumour or after injury.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I perform most of these operations under local anaesthesia through a small incision. The main rule is not to wait until the muscles start wasting: after that, recovery may be incomplete.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>Sudden numbness or weakness of the arm and leg on one side, a drooping face, speech problems — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong> If after an injury or a cut your fingers stop moving or lose sensation — go to an emergency department and see a neurosurgeon the same day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
